= Filling =

Place a sheet of kitchen foil on the middle oven shelf and preheat to 180°C.

Rub the @sweet potato{1%large}(250 g) with half a teaspoon of olive oil and pierce the skin several times. Bake on the top oven shelf for ~{1%hour}.

= Dipping Sauce =

Mix the @Greek yoghurt{200%g}, @lemon juice{1%tbsp}, @garlic{1%clove}(crushed), @ground coriander{1%tsp}, and @mint, dried or fresh{pinch} together in a small bowl, adding a splash of water if it is too thick. Season to taste and set aside.

= Dough =

Mix the @chapati flour{250%g} and @salt{0.5%tsp} in a large bowl. Make a well in the middle and gradually pour in @water{160%ml}, mixing together to form a rough dough. Turn out onto a lightly floured surface and knead for ~{5%minutes}, until a smooth elastic ball of dough forms. Cover with an upturned bowl or a clean tea towel and leave to rest for ~{20%minutes}.

= Assemble Flatbreads =

Heat a splash of the @olive oil{} in a #large frying pan{}. Fry the @leek{1%large}(thinly sliced) and @garlic{2%cloves}(crushed or grated) gently for ~{5%minutes} until soft. Tip into a large bowl. Scoop the cooked sweet potato from its skin and place into the bowl with the @frozen spinach{200%g}(defrosted), @chilli flakes{pinch}, and any @?leftover herbs{}(e.g. parsley, coriander, or chives). Season to taste with @black pepper{}(freshly ground).

Divide the dough into 4 equal pieces and roll each into a thin round, roughly 20 cm in diameter. Spoon a quarter of the sweet potato mixture onto one side of a piece dough, brush the edge with water and fold the opposite edge over to enclose the filling. Press the edges together to seal, making sure there’s no air trapped inside. Repeat with the remaining dough and filling.

Heat a little olive oil in the large frying pan over a medium heat and fry the bolani for ~{2%minutes} on each side, until crisp and golden. Drain on kitchen paper and serve with the sauce for dipping.